#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "522a785c-3e1e-5515-82c9-8980b448bee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10553" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ad02a19-7b07-5e07-8388-b46fd1cc48ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10554" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "37af544e-feab-52b0-bcef-887c723093b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10764" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27eb827-5889-5235-9bae-d6c309c0f5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189434} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fe5d8f-33d8-56cb-bf17-65f6defef94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189435} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae381a4-c807-564c-bf13-80830481f58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189436} {:answer/id #uuid "201e2ae7-1d3a-541f-a950-972540b81ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189437} {:answer/id #uuid "4d361e7a-1c99-54a6-8471-22aba0b236d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d361e7a-1c99-54a6-8471-22aba0b236d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189438} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a0de0-58ce-563f-bc1d-7a5688015c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189433}] :question/id #uuid "d4a29a66-652c-5a42-830a-8d138db1036c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100494" :question/problem "What is $∫_{- ∞}^{0}x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x^{3}}dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcd669c5-15c8-58a2-8b64-7ef90c4a3660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301313} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4915c4-b96b-5786-9e1d-7b9cba4d0491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} {:answer/id #uuid "ba37b007-40c8-5909-9627-1e2981b3f99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301315} {:answer/id #uuid "10c5eeb2-e0fc-5da5-95da-ed99d9cc86e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301316} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9b5cac-d412-5e11-a273-42a30a5cebd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4915c4-b96b-5786-9e1d-7b9cba4d0491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301314} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaae668d-9b89-5711-9627-f309743a340a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "969bf2aa-8cb3-5087-a3e3-258b63062534" :question/math-academy-id "q-152875" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fe1340-759b-586c-837e-bd624a59f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06fe1340-759b-586c-837e-bd624a59f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5e^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301345} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f2aa46-8db9-554e-a213-55c6023dfb62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0191a30-c2e2-52a8-94ee-6284596521e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350830" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4a}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/ade58ce56684420fb0f00236af50bbb54f76801fc8870e1cce135cbd51f1b726.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }10{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-4a}) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
